/var/home/charlie/.hermes/webui/sessions/781a8599e120.json:710:      "reasoning": "Now I have a clearer picture. Let me analyze the findings:\n\n1. **ADB connectivity**: NetBird (`100.87.37.3:5555`) works, WiFi (`192.168.123.22:5555`) works.\n\n2. **NetBird status**: \n   - `tun0` exists, route exists\n   - `LazyConnectionEnabled: false`, `isConnectionForceRelayed: false`\n   - Ping to `100.87.171.39` is good: 21ms, 0% loss\n   - 8787 and 5000 reachable via NetBird\n\n3. **WiFi status**:\n   - Connected to \"PDCN\", BSSID `fc:fc:43:26:60:b8`\n   - Signal strength -33dBm (good), 104Mbps, 11n, channel 2412\n   - IP: `192.168.123.22/24`\n   - Default route goes through `wlan0`\n\n4. **mihomo status**:\n   - Running on `7890/7891/7892`, DNS on `5354`\n   - Global proxy set to `127.0.0.1:7890`\n   - `ss -tlnp` shows mihomo listening on 7890/7891/7892/5354\n   - `adb` also has ports 8787/5000/8648 open (from adbd, which makes sense for ADB port forwarding or the adbd daemon)\n\n5. **Critical finding - name resolution vs IP**:\n   - `curl http://100.87.171.39:8787/api/sessions` = **200, 0.20s** ✅\n   - `curl http://charlie1990.duckdns.org:8787/api/sessions` = **502, 3.3s** ❌\n   - Direct IP access works fine via WiFi!\n   \n6. **TLS/curl issue**:\n   - `curl https://www.google.com/generate_204` with explicit proxy returns: `TLS connect error: OPENSSL_internal:invalid library (0)` — this is the known Android curl SSL library conflict\n   - `curl -x http://127.0.0.1:7890 https://www.google.com` also fails with the same TLS error\n   - `curl https://www.alipay.com` returns 200 (works)\n   - `curl https://www.douban.com` via proxy = TLS error, direct = 000 timeout\n   \n   So the TLS issue is specifically with HTTPS via proxy, while direct HTTP and some HTTPS sites work. This matches the known \"Android curl OPENSSL_internal\" issue.\n\n7. **UID curl test failure**:\n   - `su <uid> -c curl` commands all returned 000 timeout for both hermes and google\n   - This was because the `for u in 10414 10336 ...` syntax was wrong — the `id` command output was empty (since the packages aren't debuggable), so `$u` was empty\n   - Need to fix this test\n\n8. **Phone network stability script**:\n   - Ran successfully and showed everything OK under NetBird\n   - WiFi also shows Hermes direct IP reachable\n\n9. **Current state**:\n   - User is on WiFi (PDCN), default route via wlan0\n   - NetBird is connected with tun0 up\n   - Phone can reach Hermes at `100.87.171.39:8787` directly in 0.2s\n   - Phone CANNOT reach Hermes at `charlie1990.duckdns.org:8787` (502 in 3.3s) — but this is likely a DuckDNS/FRP issue, not phone issue\n   - Proxy HTTPS curl has known TLS library conflict\n   - `latest.json` is stale (July 28), phone network monitor scripts are failing\n\n10. **What's actually broken from the phone's perspective**:\n    - DNS resolution for `charlie1990.duckdns.org` works (connects, gets 502, not NXDOM)\n    - But the 502 on DuckDNS while direct IP works suggests the DuckDNS -> Fedora path is broken at the router/FRP level, not the phone\n    - The user's complaint is vague (\"网络有问题\"), but from the phone:\n      - NetBird path to Hermes IP: ✅ works\n      - WiFi direct IP to Hermes: ✅ works\n      - WiFi DuckDNS name to Hermes: ❌ 502 (but this is same as the known router issue from memory)\n      - HTTPS through proxy: ⚠️ TLS library conflict (known, affects curl only)\n      - Browser UID-based curl via proxy: ⚠️ same TLS issue\n\nLet me check if the DuckDNS issue is the same as the user's known router issue from memory:\n- Memory says: \"Local/LAN `19899/19000/19881` are healthy and router->LAN `19899` works, but DuckDNS/IPv4 `charlie1990.duckdns.org:19899` returns `502`\"\n- But here we're testing port 8787, not 19899\n\nLet me check the FRP status and DuckDNS for port 8787. Actually the user said \"一加手机网络有问题\" which is vague. I should check if they mean the phone's general network, or Hermes access from phone.\n\nGiven that:\n1. NetBird path works\n2. WiFi direct IP to Hermes works\n3. DuckDNS name returns 502\n4. Proxy HTTPS curl has known TLS issue\n\nThe main issues I can identify and potentially fix:\n1. The DuckDNS/FRP 502 issue for Hermes — but this is a router/FRP issue, not phone-specific\n2. The stale `latest.json` — can be refreshed\n3. The phone-side scripts with stale IPs — need to grep and fix\n\nWait, I should also check what the user actually experiences. Let me check if the 502 on DuckDNS is specific to port 8787 or if 19976 works. Also check if the phone can access Hermes via the public URL.\n\nActually, looking back at the output: `hermes_name_proxy=502 3.443069s` — this was with `-x http://127.0.0.1:7890`. Let me test without proxy too.\n\nLet me also check: the phone's WiFi DNS. The `getprop` didn't show DNS directly. Let me check `getprop net.dns1` etc.\n\nActually, let me focus on what I can fix and what the actual user-perceptible problem is. I should ask the user what specifically isn't working, OR give them a clear status and fix what I can.\n\nGiven the user said \"请检查\", I should:\n1. Give current status\n2. Identify clear issues\n3. Fix auto-fixable ones\n4. Ask about specific symptoms if still ambiguous\n\nLet me check DuckDNS public paths and FRP status on the host side.",
